  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Federal contract obligations for Custom Computer Programming Services (NAICS 541511) are $7.4B in FY2026 to date. Obligations are down 27.6% year over year. The market has recorded 4,292 awards so far, with an average award size of $1.7M. Historical obligations show a rise from $8.6B in FY21 to a peak of $10.8B in FY24, then $10.3B in FY25, and now $7.4B in FY26. The Department of Defense accounts for the largest share of FY2026 to date obligations at $3.2B, followed by the Department of Health and Human Services at $1B and the Department of Veterans Affairs at $746M. On the vendor side, PALANTIR USG INC leads with $892M, followed by GENERAL DYNAMICS INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY, INC. at $568M and BOOZ ALLEN HAMILTON INC at $326M. These figures are fiscal-year-to-date contract obligations and represent the current snapshot of federal buying in NAICS 541511.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How big is the market right now?",
        "body": "Total obligations are $7.4B in FY2026 to date. There have been 4,292 awards with an average award size of $1.7M. The fiscal run of prior years shows $10.3B in FY25 and $10.8B in FY24, indicating larger totals in recent full fiscal years."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Who dominates buying and who wins the work?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the largest buyer with $3.2B in obligations in FY2026 to date, followed by the Department of Health and Human Services at $1B, and the Department of Veterans Affairs at $746M. Top vendors by obligations are PALANTIR USG INC at $892M, GENERAL DYNAMICS INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY, INC. at $568M, and BOOZ ALLEN HAMILTON INC at $326M."
      }
    ]
  },
